From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 00:30:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] EXPERIMENT: drm: h713: expose a render node to unblock VA-API interop

mpv plays hardware-decoded video today with --hwdec=vaapi-copy, but the
zero-copy --hwdec=vaapi path fails at "Could not create a VA display", and the
cause is not the VA driver. The same driver and environment create a display
fine under vo=drm and under vo=gpu with vaapi-copy.

It is this device having no render node. mpv's DRM/EGL context calls
drmGetRenderDeviceNameFromFd() on the DISPLAY device to find a render node for
VA-API interop:

  [vo/gpu/opengl] Could not find path to render node.
  [vo/gpu/vaapi]  Trying to open a drm VA display...
  [vo/gpu/vaapi]  Could not create a VA display.

  /dev/dri/by-path/platform-5600000.display-card -> card0     (this driver)
  /dev/dri/by-path/platform-1800000.gpu-render   -> renderD128 (panfrost)

card0 has only card0 and controlD64. The render node mpv should use belongs to
panfrost, and mpv offers no option to point it there -- --vaapi-device is
honoured for the copy path and ignored for interop.

So expose a render node here. It carries only the GEM and PRIME ioctls; the
core keeps dumb-buffer ioctls master-only. libva does not care what the fd is,
because v4l2_request talks to /dev/media* rather than to DRM, so any valid fd
is enough to get past display creation.

BE CLEAR ABOUT WHAT THIS IS. DRIVER_RENDER on a display-only device advertises
a capability the hardware does not have. It is a workaround for a userspace
assumption -- that a display device's render node is the right one to use --
and the honest fix lives in mpv, or in using a Wayland compositor with
dmabuf-wayland instead. It is tried here because it is small, reversible, and
settles empirically whether the render node is really the only thing in the way.

THE RISK, and the thing to check before keeping it: mesa currently pairs this
display-only card with panfrost's render node by itself, which is what makes
vo=gpu work at all (GL_RENDERER='Mali-G31 (Panfrost)'). A render node appearing
on this device could change that pairing. If vo=gpu stops reaching Mali-G31,
this costs more than it buys and should be dropped.

OUT OF SERIES.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
@@ -504,7 +504,28 @@
 DEFINE_DRM_GEM_DMA_FOPS(h713_afbd_fops);
 
 static const struct drm_driver h713_afbd_driver = {
-	.driver_features	= DRIVER_GEM | DRIVER_MODESET | DRIVER_ATOMIC,
+	/*
+	 * DRIVER_RENDER on a display-only device is deliberate and is a
+	 * userspace workaround, not a claim about the hardware. mpv's DRM/EGL
+	 * context calls drmGetRenderDeviceNameFromFd() on the DISPLAY device to
+	 * find a render node for VA-API interop; this device has none, so mpv
+	 * logs "Could not find path to render node", leaves render_fd unset and
+	 * then cannot create a VA display -- which is what blocks zero-copy
+	 * --hwdec=vaapi. The render node it should use belongs to panfrost, and
+	 * mpv offers no option to point it there.
+	 *
+	 * With this flag the device gains a render node exposing only the GEM
+	 * and PRIME ioctls (dumb-buffer ioctls stay master-only, as the core
+	 * enforces). libva does not care what the fd is -- v4l2_request talks to
+	 * /dev/media*, not to DRM -- so any valid fd unblocks it.
+	 *
+	 * The risk being tested is mesa: it currently pairs this display-only
+	 * card with panfrost's render node by itself (kmsro), and a render node
+	 * appearing here could change that pairing and break the working
+	 * vo=gpu path. Verify vo=gpu still reaches Mali-G31 before keeping this.
+	 */
+	.driver_features	= DRIVER_GEM | DRIVER_MODESET | DRIVER_ATOMIC |
+				  DRIVER_RENDER,
 	.fops			= &h713_afbd_fops,
 	DRM_GEM_DMA_DRIVER_OPS,
 	DRM_FBDEV_DMA_DRIVER_OPS,
